---
title: Your first tenant
description: Creating the isolation boundary that everything else lives inside.
section: Getting started
---

A tenant is the container for a population of users and the applications they sign into. You need
one before you can do anything else.

## Create it

From the sidebar's tenant switcher, choose **Create Tenant** (or visit
`/tenants/new` on the dashboard origin). A fresh organization drops you straight here, since there is nothing
else to look at.

Creating a tenant generates its **ES256 signing keypair** on the spot. That keypair is what makes
the tenant an OIDC issuer: it signs every token issued to every user in this tenant, and it is
published at the tenant's JWKS endpoint so your applications can verify those tokens without
calling back to GateKeeper.

## The fields

| Field | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| **Name** | Display name, shown in the switcher. |
| **Description** | Free text, for your own benefit. |
| **Active** | Turning this off disables sign-in for every user in the tenant. |
| **Self-registration** | Whether end users may create their own accounts from the hosted sign-up page. Off means users can only be created by an admin. |
| **Self-service password reset** | Whether end users may use "forgot password". Off means only an admin can reset a password. |

The two self-service switches are the interesting ones. They are the difference between a
consumer-style product (anyone can sign up, anyone can recover their account) and an internal tool
(accounts are provisioned by an administrator and nobody else).

> [!NOTE]
> Self-service password reset needs a working SMTP provider, or the reset mail has nowhere to go.
> Configure one under [Email](/docs/email) before you turn it on.

## One tenant or several?

Use a separate tenant when the users are genuinely a separate population:

- **Yes:** staging and production. You do not want a staging account to be able to sign into
  production, and separate signing keys guarantee it.
- **Yes:** two unrelated products with unrelated user bases.
- **No:** "admins" and "customers" of the same product. They are the same population with different
  permissions — model that with [roles and groups](/docs/users-groups-roles).

Splitting a tenant later is painful: users, credentials, and MFA enrolments do not move between
tenants. Deciding this correctly now is worth the thought.

## Your tenant's OIDC endpoints

Once the tenant exists, it is a fully-formed issuer. These are the URLs your applications will use
(and the two your libraries will discover on their own):

```http
GET /.well-known/openid-configuration
GET /.well-known/jwks.json
```

Discovery advertises exactly what this provider supports — `response_types_supported: ["code"]`,
`code_challenge_methods_supported: ["S256"]` — which is the subject of the
[next page](/docs/integrate-your-app).
